european lawmakers are set to discuss  the fate of migrants  stranded along the eu's external borders  today  the situation is especially acute in  bosnia where  thousands of mostly middle eastern  migrants remain in limbo  bossier is not an eu member but it  borders croatia  which is in the union human rights  groups say migrants have frequently  reported rough encounters with croatian  police  including beatings and being forcefully  sent back into bosnia  croatian authorities deny the  allegations currently there are nearly 9  000 migrants estimated to be in bosnia  many are from afghanistan pakistan or  syria the un's migration agency says  more than a third are living without any  shelter at all  many were left homeless when a camp in  lieber was evacuated in december  after bosnian authorities failed to  prepare the camp for winter  the empty camp was later set on fire the  bosnian army has set up new tents but  ngos  say more permanent shelter is sorely  needed marina strauss reports  for the past three months this makeshift  camp near bosnia's border with croatia  has been home for rahil suffer the young  pakistani shares a shelter in the woods  with five others it's my sleeping bag  this potato chili  water everything there but you know  onion  this is my food they have enough to eat  saffron says  but other than that they have  practically nothing  all this time of year temperatures often  drop well below zero  suffer has to collect wood from the  forest to keep them warm  there's only one reason the men enter  these conditions  they want to get to croatia and into the  european union  try it maybe 10 15 time i'll go but  police catch  they beat why you come in the crochet  why are you cause across water  you are illegal you don't have documents  why you coming across the border  that's why too much try but inshallah  one day successfully  no problem zafur wants to find work in  germany  in his hometown of karachi pakistan he  says he had to struggle  day in day out just to make ends meet  and while living in the woods is hard he  says it's still better than life in  lieber  the lipa refugee camp made headlines in  recent weeks  first it was cleared and then burnt down  the migrants and refugees have now been  moved into tents  but many here say nobody should live  like this in winter  it's pictures like these that have  attracted unwanted attention to how  bosnian authorities  have handled the situation but the  region's prime minister says  the eu and the bosnian government share  the blame  there are solutions for the migration  situation in bosnia  the highest officials in this country  need to show political will to stop and  regulate  illegal migration into the country and  to control how the money is spent  of all the people we spoke to here none  want to stay in bosnia  but rather to continue their journey  into the eu  for many the chances of being granted  asylum in the eu  are slim but it doesn't deter suffer who  says  going back to pakistan is not an option  europe have everything they give camp is  good  every food is good cloth is good  everything we work there no problem  that's why we want to go there you know  as soon as it gets a bit warmer rahi  sufferer will try to cross the border  again  but for now he has no choice but to wait  here  alive in mud rain and severe cold  dw's marina strauss who filed that  report joins us now from brussels  marine eu lawmakers due to meet to  discuss this issue today what have they  been saying about the situation there  well they're just starting the  discussion right now but we know what  you commissioned your executive body has  said about the situation also eu member  member states they have repeatedly been  saying  this is an unacceptable situation this  is a humanitarian crisis  that has to be solved immediately and  they're putting the blame on the bosnian  government saying they really have to do  something to improve the situation  the thing is there actually is a refugee  shelter in the nearby town of bihac  that has been closed down just a couple  of months ago it's not a luxury facility  it's like an old company but still it's  better than what we're seeing  in lipa camp and it was founded by the  eu but there are many people protesting  in front of this shelter we went there  and talked to these people and they say  they had problems  with migrants in the past and that's why  they don't want to  this facility to be reopened um  so that's why local authorities are  refusing to  reopen this shelter so things are a lot  more complicated than what they seem at  first sight  and you've of course just returned from  bosnia and spoken to a lot of the  migrants there what more can you tell us  about  these allegations of croatian police  brutally sending migrants back to  the bosnian border  well all migrants and all refugees  we talked to have repeated like  completed these allegations so not only  them also the local authorities  said this is happening for example  raheel safar the young pakistani man we  saw in the report he said  he tried 10-15 times to cross the border  and  each time he was beaten up by the police  sometimes they took away  his mobile phone that sometimes his  money sometimes hits close that's what  he told us but he's not  the only way the only one and they have  been there have been  numerous media reports on these issues  ngos have been  collecting photos documenting these  abuses the croatian authorities are  denying  such claims but critics are saying this  is a clear breach of international law  and also of eu regulation because  everyone who arrives at the border of an  eu country  has to be granted the right to at least  apply for asylum  dw correspondent marina strauss thanks  for that
 

